ipho-2023-t3.prompt:
The following equation holds at an arbitrary point \((x, z)\) on the water surface, \[\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{z}{\ell}\right)^{a} + \cos\theta(x) = \text{constant}.
\tag{1}
\end{equation}\] Determine the exponent \(a\) and express the constant \(\ell\) in terms of \(\gamma\) and \(\rho\) . Note that this equation holds regardless of hydrophilic or hydrophobic board materials. 0.8pt

ipho-2021-t1.prompt:
Assuming that the crust is isotropic, find how its density \(\rho\) depends on its temperature \(T\) . Assuming that \(|k_l| \ll 1\) , write your answer in the approximate form \[\begin{equation}
\rho(T) \approx \rho_1 \left[1 + k\frac{T_1 - T}{T_1 - T_0}\right], \tag{2}
\end{equation}\] where terms of order \(k_l^2\) and higher are neglected. Then, identify constant \(k\) . 0.6pt

ipho-2019-t2.prompt:
Find an expression for the time-averaged power \(\langle H(t)\rangle\) per unit volume absorbed by the water. 0.5pt The time-average for a time dependent periodic variable \(f(t)\) over its period \(T\) is defined as: \[\begin{equation}
\langle f(t)\rangle = \frac{1}{T}\int_{t_0}^{t_0+T} f(t)\,\mathrm{d}t. \tag{1}
\end{equation}\]

ipho-2019-t3.prompt:
The parcel volume \(V(x,t)\) oscillates around the equilibrium value of \(V_0 = S\Delta x\) and has the form \[\begin{equation}
V(x,t) = V_0 + V_1(x)\cos(\omega t).  \tag{2}
\end{equation}\] Obtain an expression for \(V_1(x)\) in terms of \(V_0\) , \(a\) , \(k\) and \(x\) . 0.5pt

Assume that the total pressure of the gas, as a result of the sound wave, takes the approximate form \[\begin{equation}
p(x,t) = p_0 - p_1(x)\cos(\omega t). \tag{3}
\end{equation}\] Considering the forces acting on the parcel of gas, compute the amplitude \(p_1(x)\) of the pressure oscillation to leading order, in terms of the position \(x\) , the equilibrium density \(\rho_0\) , the displacement amplitude \(a\) and the wave parameters \(k\) and \(\omega\) . 0.7pt

The change in the gas temperature due to the adiabatic expansion and contraction, as a result of the sound wave, takes the form: \[\begin{equation}
T(x,t) = T_0 - T_1(x)\cos(\omega t). \tag{4}
\end{equation}\] Compute the amplitude \(T_1(x)\) of the temperature oscillations in terms of \(T_0\) , \(\gamma\) , \(a\) , \(k\) and \(x\) . 0.7pt

Consider a specific parcel of gas in the region of the stack, originally at \(x_0 = L/4\) . As the parcel moves within the stack, the local temperature of the nearby part of the stack changes as follows: \[\begin{equation}
T_\mathrm{env}(t) = T_0 - T_\mathrm{st}\cos(\omega t). \tag{5}
\end{equation}\] Express \(T_\mathrm{st}\) in terms of \(a\) , \(\tau\) and \(\ell\) . 0.4pt

In order to calculate work, we will consider a change to the volume of the moving parcel as a result of the thermal contact with the stack. Let us write the pressure and the volume of the parcel under the stack’s influence in the form: \[\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
p &= p_0 + p_a\sin(\omega t) - p_b\cos(\omega t), \\
V &= V_0 + V_a\sin(\omega t) + V_b\cos(\omega t).
\end{aligned} \tag{6}
\end{equation}\] Given \(p_a\) and \(p_b\) , find the coefficients \(V_a\) and \(V_b\) . Express your answer in terms of \(p_a\) , \(p_b\) , \(p_0\) , \(V_0\) , \(\gamma\) , \(\tau\) , \(\tau_\mathrm{cr}\) , \(\beta\) , \(\omega\) , \(a\) and \(\ell\) . 1.9pt

ipho-2018-t1.prompt:
Consider a system of two stars with masses \(M_1\) , \(M_2\) , at locations \(\vec{r}_1\) , \(\vec{r}_2\) , respectively, with respect to the center-of-mass of the system, that is, \[\begin{equation}
M_1\vec{r}_1 + M_2\vec{r}_2 = 0\,. \tag{1}
\end{equation}\] The stars are isolated from the rest of the Universe and moving at non-relativistic velocities. Using Newton’s laws, the acceleration vector of mass \(M_1\) can be expressed as \[\begin{equation}
\frac{\mathrm{d}^2\vec{r}_1}{\mathrm{d}t^2} = -\alpha\frac{\vec{r}_1}{r_1^n}\,, \tag{2}
\end{equation}\] where \(r_1 = |\vec{r}_1|\) , \(r_2 = |\vec{r}_2|\) . Find \(n \in \mathbb{N}\) and \(\alpha = \alpha(G, M_1, M_2)\) , where \(G\) is Newton’s constant [ \(G \simeq 6.67 \times 10^{-11}\,\mathrm{N\,m^2\,kg^{-2}}\) ]. 1.0pt

The total energy of the 2-mass system, in circular orbits, can be expressed as: \[\begin{equation}
E = A(\mu, \Omega, L) - G\frac{M\mu}{L}\,, \tag{3}
\end{equation}\] where \[\begin{equation}
\mu \equiv \frac{M_1 M_2}{M_1 + M_2}\,, \qquad M \equiv M_1 + M_2\,, \tag{4}
\end{equation}\] are the reduced mass and total mass of the system, \(\Omega\) is the angular velocity of each mass and \(L\) is the total separation \(L = r_1 + r_2\) . Obtain the explicit form of the term \(A(\mu, \Omega, L)\) . 1.0pt

For the circular orbits described in A.2 the components of \(Q_{ij}\) can be expressed as a function of time \(t\) as: \[\begin{equation}
Q_{ii} = \frac{\mu L^2}{2}\left(a_i + b_i \cos kt\right)\,, \qquad Q_{ij} \overset{i\neq j}{=} \frac{\mu L^2}{2}\, c_{ij} \sin kt\,. \tag{8}
\end{equation}\] Determine \(k\) in terms of \(\Omega\) and the numerical values of the constants \(a_i\) , \(b_i\) , \(c_{ij}\) . 1.0pt

Compute the power \(\mathcal{P}\) emitted in gravitational waves for that system, and obtain: \[\begin{equation}
\mathcal{P} = \xi\frac{G}{c^5}\mu^2 L^4 \Omega^6\,. \tag{9}
\end{equation}\] What is the number \(\xi\) ? [If you could not obtain \(\xi\) , use \(\xi = 6.4\) in the following.] 1.0pt

In the absence of GW emission the two masses will orbit on a fixed circular orbit indefinitely. However, the emission of GWs causes the system to lose energy and to slowly evolve towards smaller circular orbits. Obtain that the rate of change \(\frac{\mathrm{d}\Omega}{\mathrm{d}t}\) of the orbital angular velocity takes the form \[\begin{equation}
\left(\frac{\mathrm{d}\Omega}{\mathrm{d}t}\right)^3 = (3\xi)^3\frac{\Omega^{11}}{c^{15}}\left(GM_\mathrm{c}\right)^5\,, \tag{10}
\end{equation}\] where \(M_\mathrm{c}\) is called the chirp mass . Obtain \(M_\mathrm{c}\) as a function of \(M\) and \(\mu\) . This mass determines the increase in frequency during the orbital decay. [The name "chirp" is inspired by the high pitch sound (increasing frequency) produced by small birds.] 1.0pt

Using the information provided above, relate the orbital angular velocity \(\Omega\) with the GW frequency \(f_\mathrm{GW}\) . Knowing that, for any smooth function \(F(t)\) and \(a \neq 1\) , \[\begin{equation}
\frac{\mathrm{d}F(t)}{\mathrm{d}t} = \chi F(t)^a \quad\Rightarrow\quad F(t)^{1-a} = \chi(1-a)(t - t_0)\,, \tag{11}
\end{equation}\] where \(\chi\) is a constant and \(t_0\) is an integration constant, show that (10) implies that the GW frequency is \[\begin{equation}
f_\mathrm{GW}^{-8/3} = 8\pi^{8/3}\xi\left(\frac{GM_c}{c^3}\right)^{(2/3)+p}(t_0 - t)^{2-p} \tag{12}
\end{equation}\] and determine the constant \(p\) . 2.0pt

From the figure, estimate \(f_\mathrm{GW}(t)\) at \[\begin{equation}
t_{\overline{\mathrm{AB}}} = \frac{t_\mathrm{B} + t_\mathrm{A}}{2} \quad\text{and}\quad t_{\overline{\mathrm{CD}}} = \frac{t_\mathrm{D} + t_\mathrm{C}}{2}\,. \tag{13}
\end{equation}\] Assuming that (12) is valid all the way until the collision (which strictly speaking is not true) and that the two objects have equal mass, estimate the chirp mass, \(M_c\) , and total mass of the system, in terms of solar masses \(M_\odot \simeq 2 \times 10^{30}\) kg. 1.0pt

